{
  "analysis": "The review mainly proposes new features rather than reporting harmful mechanics: auctions, changing obstacle-removal behavior, a multi-account widget, and showing advanced troop statistics. These requested features themselves are not codable under R12. However, the request to see \"how much time is left for upgrades\" explicitly describes existing upgrade timers, which impose waiting before upgrades complete. That is a wait-to-play mechanic under Playing by Appointment. No web search is needed because the relevant mechanic is clear from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "see how much time is left for upgrades",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that make players wait for something; the boundary rule includes a build timer.",
      "rationale": "The span describes upgrade completion timers that require the player to wait according to the game's schedule."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based wheel reward mechanic. After the wheel appears to stop on a valuable prize, the game allegedly changes the result to another spin, producing a much lower reward. This explicitly attributes the outcome to deliberate manipulation by the game rather than ordinary bad luck, supporting Illusion of Control. The wheel also delivers rewards through an unpredictable spin-based system without a monetary stake being described, supporting Reward Mania but not Gambling.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts that the game manipulates the determination of outcomes.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer claims the game changes an apparent winning wheel result into a lower-value result, alleging manipulated rather than genuinely determined outcomes."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners; chance-based delivery systems such as spin wheels without an explicitly described monetary stake.",
      "rationale": "The game delivers prizes through an unpredictable spin-wheel mechanism, and no paid stake is described."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review primarily describes technical faults: crashes prevent opening gifts and trading, lag prevents throwing Poké Balls and makes battles unresponsive, and freezing allows the opponent to continue attacking. These malfunctions do not establish dark-pattern mechanics under R11. The review does explicitly mention PvP competition against an opponent, which is an in-game competitive mechanic independent of the malfunction, so Competition applies.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponent keep attacking",
      "rule_applied": "definition: The game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes an opponent attacking the reviewer in a PvP battle, establishing player-versus-player competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive battles against other players, including repeated use of powerful cards and card-spamming that makes defense difficult. It explicitly claims that spending money provides an advantage and that skill alone is insufficient to advance, which supports Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. The reviewer also describes deliberately annoying play as a way to win, indicating an incentive to use harmful or aggravating conduct for advantage. The updates buff cards that players dislike, but the review does not say that previously purchased items lost value, so Power Creep is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against other players using particular cards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The review states that spending money provides an advantage needed to advance to higher progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior",
      "span": "you have to go out of your way to be annoying to actually win in a game",
      "rule_applied": "definition; harmful conduct is described as producing an in-game advantage.",
      "rationale": "The span describes deliberately aggravating other players as a strategy for gaining victory."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a game rule involving 9 diamonds and a game element that is expected to appear within a limited number of moves, but it does not clearly describe waiting, payment for progress, or real-money conversion into diamonds. The reviewer mentions a pass as an existing purchase option, which supports Recurring Fee because battle passes are an indicator of that label. The reviewer also explicitly describes trading lives with friends and a team, which is a resource exchange between existing players and supports Reciprocity. Merely suggesting friends and a team does not establish recruitment pressure or social obligation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes are an indicator of Recurring Fee.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies a pass as an existing paid game offering, which is covered by the battle-pass indicator."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "Reciprocity applies where the player exchanges resources, lives, or favours with other players.",
      "rationale": "Trading lives is an explicit resource exchange that can create an obligation to reciprocate."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current game mechanics. First, obtaining a new life is controlled by an in-game waiting period, implied by the request for “only 5-10 minutes for a new life.” This is an arbitrary wait timer that restricts when the player can continue. Second, the game provides daily prizes and makes the player start over after missing a day, which is a daily-return incentive with an active penalty. The harder levels and the game's addictiveness are evaluations, not sufficient mechanics for additional labels. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait; the span implies a timed wait before a new life becomes available.",
      "rationale": "The player must wait for a new life before continuing, so gameplay availability is governed by an in-game timer."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars with an active consequence for missing a day.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily prizes and resets the player's progress in that reward sequence when they miss a day."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement screen that obstructs the player’s return to gameplay. The ad has no visible close control, and tapping elsewhere redirects the player to the store, so the opt-out path is absent or misdirected. Although the reviewer says they must kill the game to leave the screen, no progress loss or inability to end a session under the game’s rules is described. Merely seeing or being stuck on an ad does not establish compelled or rewarded ad viewing under T_Advertisement.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "there is no way to close it. No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store.",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs UI complaint: assign where the interface obstructs an opt-out, including an absent close control or a control that routes the player to the opposite action.",
      "rationale": "The advertisement screen removes the normal opt-out and redirects taps toward the store instead of closing the ad."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes strategy gameplay involving building and expanding an empire, plus real-time battles against other players. The player-versus-player combat explicitly establishes competition. The positive descriptions of graphics, storyline, immersion, and strategic depth are evaluations or ordinary game features and do not support additional dark-pattern labels.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "indicator: The game makes you compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes real-time gameplay in which the player battles other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progression being contingent on purchasing something because the game provides no extra energy. The purchase is presented as necessary to have any chance of progressing, which is a payment gate on advancement. Although energy is mentioned, the review does not describe an energy regeneration timer or waiting schedule, so Playing by Appointment is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay Wall indicator",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly states that purchasing is necessary to make progress, indicating that further advancement is contingent on payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits continued play: once the energy is depleted, the player cannot meaningfully progress until the energy regenerates. It also explicitly describes a long recovery timer, making this an imposed wait before progression can resume. The comments about there being little to do, wanting console support, a better storyline, bigger maps, and more customization are requests or general evaluations, not coded mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players wait; the boundary rule requires the span to describe the wait or regeneration schedule, such as energy refilling over time.",
      "rationale": "Depleted energy blocks further progress and the player must wait for the recovery timer to refill it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes very slow advancement through hard levels, saying it takes a week to pass them, but it does not identify a repetitive activity required for progress, so this alone does not establish Grinding. It also describes tasks as effectively impossible unless the player buys boosters, implying that payment provides the route past a progression obstacle. The review does not describe a recurring schedule, a daily-return reward, or a game-wide unattainable end state.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Free alternative boundary rule and Pay Wall indicator: payment is presented as necessary to overcome an obstacle and continue progress.",
      "rationale": "The span implies that progressing through the tasks is not feasible without purchasing boosters."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event with a reward that was nearly achieved, but the event changed after the player stopped playing overnight despite displaying two days remaining. This makes the event's game-controlled schedule determine when the reward opportunity is available and causes the player to lose access before the displayed window ends. The review does not explicitly describe anxiety about missing out, repetitive work, a payment requirement, or an in-game ad. The mention of the F2P model is a general evaluation and does not establish a monetization mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event's availability and associated reward are controlled by the game's schedule, and the opportunity changed while the player was away despite the displayed remaining time."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a social-recruitment mechanic that rewards inviting friends and getting them to join through Facebook, even though the reviewer says those rewards have stopped appearing. That supports Forced Fellowship because recruitment of new players is tied to in-game rewards. The reviewer also describes spending dice on a maximum bet and receiving no chance-based outcomes such as shutdowns or bank heists; this is an in-game wager using earned currency, which supports Gambling. The duplicate stickers alone do not establish a sufficiently clear randomised reward-delivery mechanic or a compulsion to complete the collection. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not support a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator: players are incentivized to invite friends to join the game and continue spreading the invitation.",
      "rationale": "The game ties rewards to recruiting new players through the reviewer's friends and Facebook network."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists.",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling definition and boundary rule: a chance mechanic with a wager using paid or earned in-game currency qualifies even when real money is not directly mentioned.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes staking dice on a maximum bet for chance-based outcomes, namely shutdowns and bank heists."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a deliberate interface default that automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-stakes table rather than the lowest-risk option. This directs the player's attention and input toward the costly table and is said to cause accidental mis-clicks that make players lose their accumulated in-game coins. The reviewer further states that, after those losses, players are forced to buy more coins with real money. The interface behavior supports UI Misdirection. The real-money purchase of replacement in-game coins supports Intermediate Currency, and the stated need to buy coins to continue playing supports Pay to Progress. The review does not explicitly establish a chance-based wager, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins. This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect and the boundary rule for attention-directing or obstructed opt-out: the interface automatically selects and focuses the costly option, causing unintended input.",
      "rationale": "The interface deliberately defaults attention and selection to the highest-bet table, making accidental mis-clicks more likely."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency: real money is used to acquire in-game currency; the transaction direction is buying coins with real money.",
      "rationale": "The phrase describes purchasing replacement in-game coins with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Free alternative boundary: payment is described as necessary after the loss of the in-game resource in order to continue playing.",
      "rationale": "After the interface-induced coin loss, the reviewer says players must pay for more coins, making continued play dependent on spending."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a hidden-outcome manipulation: they believe the game's RNG is deliberately unfavorable, rather than merely reporting an unlucky streak. The review also describes poor reward economics at around level 100, where a level costs 2200 and winning barely returns that amount, but this is a low-value reward complaint rather than a stated payment gate, currency shortfall, or repetitive progression requirement. No web search is needed because RNG is clear from context.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts that the game manipulates or determines outcomes through a hidden schedule or fixed disadvantage.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game's random outcome system is deliberately biased against players, indicating manipulated outcomes rather than ordinary bad luck."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game the reviewer experiences as a repetitive grinder, but it does not name a repeated task or activity, so that general characterization does not independently trigger Grinding. The review explicitly says that winning events is effectively unavailable to ordinary players without spending, and reports spending 30k to win the last event. This describes payment providing an advantage or serving as the route to event success, which matches Pay to Progress through its Pay to Win indicator. No separate Competition label is assigned because the review does not explicitly describe competition against other players.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event. Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money; Pay to Progress includes payment used to make progress or win in-game.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly links spending money with winning events and states that a general player cannot win without that paid advantage."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. It explicitly says the game is pay-to-win, which indicates that spending money grants gameplay advantages. Base upgrades use increasingly long update timers, imposing waits on progress. The statement that everything scales endlessly and there is no point to look forward to describes an expanding game with no attainable end state, fitting Infinite Treadmill. The reviewer calls the experience more grind, but does not identify a specific repetitive task required for progress, so that alone does not support Grinding. The UI is described as deliberately confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases, which supports UI Misdirection because the interface obstructs informed choice and directs the player toward buying. The alliance section describes coercive social grouping for protection, but the codebook excludes coerced grouping where no obligation to group members is described; it also does not clearly establish competition or harmful conduct being rewarded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly characterizes the game as pay-to-win, meaning payment provides an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that impose arbitrary wait times, including build timers.",
      "rationale": "Base updates are governed by increasingly long in-game timers that delay continued progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to, just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that continually expand the game to never allow players to complete; no attainable end state.",
      "rationale": "The game is described as scaling endlessly without a meaningful completion point."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection definition and boundary: interface design that purposefully focuses attention or obstructs informed opt-out, especially when tied to purchasing.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer attributes confusing and overwhelming interface design to steering players toward purchases."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a daily-reward system whose rewards cannot be completed or collected unless the player first bets 50,000. The timed rewards provide at most 40,000, creating a concrete gap between available rewards and the 50,000 requirement, but the review does not clearly describe real-money currency, a purchase prompt, or a progression barrier. The daily cycle is a reward condition rather than a gate on overall game progress, so Daily Rewards applies without Playing by Appointment. The betting mechanic is not coded as Gambling because no real-money or paid-currency stake is explicitly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in or daily task rewards with an active consequence or condition for obtaining the reward; the text explicitly describes daily rewards that cannot be completed without meeting the requirement.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily rewards but withholds completion of those rewards unless the player performs the required daily betting activity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a sticker-collection goal where obtaining the final one or two stickers is effectively unavailable unless the player spends a large amount of money. That makes payment an alternative required for completing the collection and makes the sticker set itself a collection target. The statements about winning and completing a goal only once in a hundred attempts express poor odds and discouragement, but they do not identify a specific chance-based wagering mechanic, random reward-delivery system, or rigged outcome. The statement about being unable to write a review without giving five stars concerns the review interface rather than an identified codebook mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay Wall / Free alternative",
      "rationale": "Completing the sticker-related goal is described as requiring substantial payment because the final items cannot otherwise be obtained."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes pursuing the final missing items in a sticker collection, implying a compulsion or goal to complete the set."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an existing player-versus-player challenge mechanic: another player can challenge them to a game. That is ordinary competition and supports S_Competition. Trading stickers is requested as a hypothetical feature, so it is not coded. The statement about purchasing a booster and using it when challenged is also framed as a desired change rather than a clearly described current mechanic. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "another player challenge me to a game",
      "rule_applied": "definition: Patterns that make players against each other in competition.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes players competing against one another through game challenges."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress becoming very slow and costly at high levels. The player repeatedly spends lives trying to beat levels and may take 5–7 days per level, which implies repeated attempts as the activity required for advancement. The review explicitly states that buying add-ons is necessary to get through the game, making payment a progression requirement. Running out of lives is mentioned, but no waiting or regeneration timer is described, so Playing by Appointment is not supported. Although the reviewer says they wasted money and time, they are uninstalling rather than describing difficulty abandoning accumulated progress, so Easy to Get, Hard to Lose is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I run out of lives 3-4 times daily to beat every alternate level, avg 5-7 days per level",
      "rule_applied": "vs Slow Progress: assign where the span describes the activity required for progress as repetitive, tedious, or requiring repeated attempts.",
      "rationale": "The player must make repeated attempts using lives over several days to advance through levels."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay Wall: the game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly says continued progress requires buying add-ons."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several current in-game monetization mechanics. Purchase offers appear immediately on login and block access to gameplay until the player deals with them, which is an in-session buying interception. Progress and competitive viability are described as dependent on spending: free-to-play players cannot progress effectively or keep up with the shifting meta, and the newest bot must be bought rather than earned. The shifting meta also devalues older bots by making newer purchases necessary to remain viable, indicating power creep. The review additionally describes free-to-play rewards being obtained through ads, implying rewarded advertisements. It does not explicitly describe a daily-return schedule, a chance-based purchase, social pressure, or a specific scarcity or collection mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game",
      "rule_applied": "Prompted-to-buy / boundary rule versus purchase solicitation: a buying prompt that directly intercepts gameplay and must be dealt with before play qualifies.",
      "rationale": "The purchase offers appear at login and interrupt access to gameplay before the player can play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck!",
      "rule_applied": "Free alternative boundary rule: payment is presented as necessary for smooth advancement, while the free route falls short.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly contrasts progression with not paying, indicating that spending is required or effectively necessary to advance."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress definition: spending money is required to obtain a gameplay-relevant item needed to progress or remain viable.",
      "rationale": "The newest bot is available through payment rather than ordinary earning, making money the route to advancement."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot",
      "rule_applied": "Power Creep definition: later releases make previously obtained gameplay items less valuable and drive new purchases.",
      "rationale": "The shifting meta makes newer bots necessary and implies that older bots lose competitive value, encouraging purchase of the newest bot."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rule_applied": "Rewarded-ad indicator: advertisements are presented as the route to obtaining benefits or rewards.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes free-to-play benefits as being tied to ads, implying incentivized advertisement viewing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a card-collection system in which card packs stop yielding new cards when the player is close to completing a set, leaving duplicate or non-progressing results. The player is pursuing a collection-completion reward, and the collection has an expiration point, so failing to complete it before that deadline causes the reward to be lost. This supports Complete the Collection and Playing by Appointment. The review does not explicitly describe random odds, paid card packs, a purchase, or anxiety about falling behind, so Gambling, Reward Mania, monetary labels, and FOMO are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "after collecting 125 cards from the beginning of the collection you will miss the reward for the set completion",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes collecting cards toward completion of a set and obtaining its completion reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "especially when the set is almost expiring",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Fear of Missing Out: where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The collection operates on a game-defined expiration schedule, and the player can lose the completion reward when the set ends."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
